This suite checks that removing an export makes every ganesha daemon of
the nfs cluster unmount it.

Each ganesha daemon mounts CephFS (libcephfs) for an export, whether or
not NFS clients use the export through that daemon. When the export is
removed, a daemon unmounts it once nothing references the export any
more, closing the mount's CephFS session. A daemon that keeps a
reference to the export keeps the mount. Its session outlives the cephx
user that mgr/nfs removes with the export, so the client cannot get
monitor maps any more and misses MDS failovers. The MDS then holds every
rank the client has a session on in up:clientreplay until it autocloses
the session (see the reclaim suite). Going by the code, such a daemon
also crashes when it shuts down: shutdown_handles() releases the handles
of the export it never freed without an op context.

The ganesha-export-rm task needs two ganesha daemons, one per host. It
creates an export, writes to it over NFS through the first daemon only,
removes it and checks that the CephFS sessions of the export's cephx user
from both daemons close within two minutes. Each job picks one setting
from qos/:

  none      no QoS: both daemons release the export
  cluster   Per_Export bandwidth control for the nfs cluster and the
            export, with Cluster QoS between the two daemons

The cluster job fails. With Cluster QoS, the daemon serving the writes
sends the export's bandwidth use to the other daemon a few times a
second. Ganesha's cluster_qos_process_pe() (MainNFSD/nfs_cluster_qos.c)
looks the export up by id with get_gsh_export() for every such message
and never puts the reference. The receiving daemon never frees the
export and keeps its CephFS mount after the removal. The daemon serving
the writes receives nothing and releases the export. On a failure the
task evicts the kept session and restarts that daemon. Its message gives
the number of Cluster QoS requests the daemon logged, the last reference
count it logged for the export and the unit's exit status from the
restart.

Cluster QoS needs a ganesha built with ENABLE_QOS and ENABLE_CLUSTER_QOS
and the peers listed in NFS_CORE_PARAM Cluster_Members, which cephadm
does not set: qos/cluster.yaml has ganesha-reconf add it to the
ganesha.conf template. The task fails early if Cluster QoS is not
running.
